Product Requirements Document
App Name:  Edith

Document Status
Complete draft. All 17 original features now have a defined behavior, and every open architecture question from the review has an answer. Remaining unknowns are the normal kind a developer surfaces once building starts, not gaps in what the app is supposed to do.

1. Overview
Edith is a mobile and desktop app for students to open, edit, annotate, organize, and convert study files in one place. The app centers on active reading and note taking on PDFs, Word documents, and images, with tools for temporary content covering, freehand drawing, and page management. Files sync between a student's phone and desktop through a device pairing code, without a traditional account or password.

2. Goals
Let a student open, mark up, and organize study materials without switching between separate apps for reading, note taking, and file conversion.
Keep files synced between a student's phone and desktop through a paired device connection, without requiring a full login system.
Give a fast way to scan physical materials into digital, searchable files.
3. Target User
The primary user is a college student who annotates study materials such as lecture slides, readings, and scanned notes. A student uses the app during study sessions on both a phone and a laptop or desktop, and needs their files and folders to match on both devices once paired.

4. Platform and Technical Scope
The app targets mobile (Android and iOS) and desktop (Windows and macOS). A cross platform framework such as Flutter can cover phone, tablet, and desktop from one codebase, though native modules are still needed for OS-specific features such as home screen widgets and the floating overlay, since each OS implements these differently. Sync between devices uses a pairing code rather than a login, generated on one device and entered on the other to link both to the same Firebase project. Firebase (Authentication and Firestore) holds the pairing record, notes, labels, and file metadata. The actual file bytes live in a separate Backblaze B2 bucket, referenced from Firestore rather than stored inside Firebase itself, since Firebase's own file storage requires a paid plan with a credit card on file. If the only paired device is lost or reset, a printed backup recovery code, generated at setup and stored separately by the student, restores access.

