6. Non-Functional Requirements
Performance: version 1 has no fixed file size cap, by decision. Files load a page at a time instead of loading the whole file into memory at once, which keeps memory use low regardless of file size. Test against one specific low end device, a few years old with limited RAM, before the freeform pane and sticker sheet features are finished, to find the real performance ceiling on target hardware. Show the student a warning once a file passes roughly 100 MB, a reasonable starting point since scanned study PDFs tend to get heavy on entry level phones past that size. This number is an estimate, not a tested fact, and should move based on what the device testing actually shows.
Security: files stored in Backblaze B2 need encryption at rest. B2 application keys must never sit inside the app itself, since a compiled app can be decompiled and any embedded key extracted. A Firebase Cloud Function should request a short lived, scoped upload authorization from B2 on the app's behalf for each upload, keeping the real key server side only. The device pairing code is the main access control in place of a login, so it needs an expiry or a way to revoke a lost device. The printed backup recovery code needs the same level of care as a password, and the app should say so clearly to the student.
Offline access: local caching of recently opened files, consistent with the local first auto save behavior.
Data privacy: storing student files and personal data falls under the Philippines Data Privacy Act of 2012 if users are based in the Philippines. Confirm the exact obligations with a legal reference before launch, since this document is not a legal review.
Accessibility: define minimum font size, contrast, and touch target size for students with visual or motor impairments, especially with freeform panes and multiple sticker sheets that can shrink very small on screen.
Backend limits: Firebase's free Spark plan caps Firestore at 1 GiB of stored data, 50,000 document reads, 20,000 writes, and 20,000 deletes per day, with 10 GiB of monthly egress, all confirmed against Firebase's own pricing page. Since Firestore only holds metadata rather than file bytes, this ceiling is unlikely to be a problem. Backblaze B2 caps free file storage at 10 GB, with free egress up to 3 times whatever is currently stored. Design around the 10 GB figure, since heavy scanned study files will reach it faster than the number suggests.
7. Assumptions
This document assumes Firebase Firestore covers the database for notes, labels, and file metadata, and that Backblaze B2 covers the actual file storage, connected through a reference stored in Firestore. It assumes the pairing code is generated on one device and entered on the other, similar to pairing a smart TV or a second browser session. It assumes OCR defaults to English unless a language setting is added later, since language scope was not specified.

8. Out of Scope for Version 1
Universal file format conversion beyond PDF, Word, and images is out of scope for version 1. Home screen and desktop widgets, and the floating window overlay, are deferred past version 1 due to the separate native work needed per OS. A traditional account and login system is out of scope, replaced by the device pairing code and its printed backup recovery code. Multi user collaboration on the same file was not in the original feature list and stays out of scope unless added later.

9. Remaining Implementation Details
These are normal build-time decisions, not open product questions. OCR language support beyond English and the exact expiry rule for the pairing code still need a technical decision once development begins.

10. Success Metrics
Suggested metrics: average time to open and annotate a file, percentage of sessions with a successful auto save, number of files converted per week, and student retention after the first week of use. These need confirmation against the actual goals for the app before they are finalized.

11. Recommended Tools
Flutter, free and open source, covers Android, iOS, Windows, and macOS from one codebase, matching the platform decision above. Visual Studio Code or Android Studio, both free, work as the editor.

For PDF viewing and annotation, the Syncfusion Flutter PDF Viewer is free under Syncfusion's Community License for anyone under 1 million dollars in yearly revenue and under 5 developers, which a student project meets. It includes virtual scrolling, matching the lazy loading recommendation in the performance section, plus built in text highlight annotations.

For OCR, google_mlkit_text_recognition is a free, MIT licensed package that runs on the device with no per scan cost, but it only works on Android and iOS, not on Windows or macOS. Tesseract OCR, also free and open source, is the standard option for the desktop side, though it needs more setup work.

For the freehand drawing in the sticker sheet and locked drawing mode, Flutter's own CustomPainter needs no extra license. The free package perfect_freehand can smooth out pen strokes if needed.

For file conversion between PDF, Word, and image, LibreOffice is free and open source, and runs in a headless, command line mode that the app calls as a background process.

Firebase handles authentication and the notes, labels, and metadata database, fully free on the Spark plan with no credit card required, as long as Firebase's own file storage product is avoided, since that specific piece is the part that needs a paid plan. Backblaze B2 handles the actual file storage instead, free up to 10 GB with no card required either. A small Firebase Cloud Function sits between the two, requesting short lived upload access from B2 so no B2 key ever ships inside the app. Figma, free tier, is useful for mocking up the freeform pane layout before it becomes real layout code. Git and GitHub cover version control for free.

As a verified student, the GitHub Student Developer Pack is worth claiming, and includes a free JetBrains license, GitHub Pro, and some cloud credit, even if this project only uses part of it.

